<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Hispania Citerior</h1><p class="st-meta">197 BCE – 27 BCE · capital Tarraco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rome's province of Nearer Spain, from the Ebro and the east coast into the Meseta, the base for Rome's long wars against the Celtiberians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hispania Citerior was created in 197 BCE as one of the two Roman provinces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, covering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>. It grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> wartime base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, established after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Scipio's</a> landing in 218 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A praetor or consul governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Carthago Nova</a>, leading near-constant campaigns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cato-the-elder">Cato</a> crushed the north-eastern revolt in 195 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius-sempronius-gracchus-consul-177-bc">Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus</a> made treaties with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians</a> in 179 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines around Carthago Nova were among the richest in the Roman world. The province steadily pushed inland, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> in 133 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>It was the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Sertorius</a>' war from 80 to 72 BCE, ended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, and of Caesar's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilerda">Ilerda</a> in 49 BCE. In 27 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus</a> enlarged it as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis</a>, adding the north-west after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabrian-wars">Cantabrian Wars</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 197 BCE – 27 BCE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:55.78%;width:3.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>197 BCE – 27 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarraco</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cato the Elder, Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus, Pompey</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nearer Spain, Hither Spain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-112&amp;focus=hispania-citerior" aria-label="Hispania Citerior on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4711 711 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hispania Citerior lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5031 967L5076 934M5031 967L5077 930M5031 967L4975 1081M5031 967L4910 1067M5031 967L4985 1015M5031 967L5029 968M5031 967L5055 941M5031 967L5075 938M5031 967L4987 920M5031 967L4978 950M5031 967L4960 913M5031 967L4919 921M5031 967L4942 924M5031 967L4901 1007M5031 967L4915 954M5031 967L4963 961M5031 967L5304 942M5031 967L5031 967